{
  "id": "Compound::DB00635 - Disease::DOID:7147",
  "verbalization": "Prednisone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is linked to Ankylosing Spondylitis through multiple indirect pathways. The lowest common ancestor of Prednisone and its related compounds, such as Methylprednisolone, is the 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid, which is also the ancestor of Therapeutic Glucocorticoids. This suggests a shared biological pathway involving steroid hormone action. The evidence indicates that Prednisone and its derivatives, through their glucocorticoid activity, may influence inflammatory processes associated with Ankylosing Spondylitis. While the direct causal link is not explicitly stated, the presence of multiple indirect pathways involving glucocorticoid receptor activation and inflammatory modulation supports a plausible mechanism by which Prednisone could contribute to the pathophysiology of the condition. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of glucocorticoids in modulating inflammation, which is a key feature of Ankylosing Spondylitis.",
